Chapter: BoundedBudgetedExceeded
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 Cost Model, the budgeted rung, the arm that must be REFUSED.

 `half` asks for a slice whose length is computed from the text it is slicing,
 so the allocation follows the input and no bound the caller states holds it.
 That is the exact pair the measurement separated: `substring line 0 4` is 16
 bytes whether the line is 64 characters or 256, and `substring line 0
 (text-length line / 2)` is 40 then 136.

 THE REFUSAL IS TRANSITIVE, so the declaration is not on the function that
 calls `substring`. `half` is undeclared and `clip` is what promises; the
 error must land on `clip` for what its callee does, the way CDX6001 does for
 `punctual`.

Section: A Callee Whose Slice Follows Its Input

  half : Text -> Text
  half (line) = substring line 0 (text-length line / 2)

Section: The Declaration That Cannot Hold

  bounded budgeted clip : Text -> Text
  clip (line) = half line

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (clip "abcdefghij")
  end
